plan FlowRuntime
title   Applications, readiness, actors and context
status  proposed
goal
    One Runtime runs every host, React tree, server request, Story and CLI command: a closed set
    of machines, one store, one ordered decision path per actor, causally complete context and
    truthful cleanup. Turns are @plan.Machines.O3, operations @plan.FlowOperations.O1, storage
    @plan.FlowPersistence.O1, React @plan.React.O1, hydration @plan.ServerRendering.O1 and lazy
    entries @plan.LazyFeatures.O1. Full notes: references/full/Runtime.full.plan.txt.
outcomes
    O1  Apps
        app({ id, machines }) is inert and lists every machine the Runtime may run. An entry is a
        Machine, { machine, context? }, or a lazy { id, load, context? } (@plan.LazyFeatures.O1).
        The list is closed for the Runtime's lifetime: no runtime registration or child actors.
        Construction throws FlowError on unknown fields, duplicate machine ids, one machine under
        two keys, and colliding operation, domain or entity identities; lazy entries are checked
        the same way when adopted, before any work. Constructing an app, setup or ref runs no
        callback, loader, service, storage access, actor, timer or evidence.

        A machine entry's context is the default wiring for every actor of that machine; a shared
        ref may override declared slots. The resolved wiring must cover every declared slot with
        a compatible provider. Call sites (ensure, create, hooks) never supply wiring. Durable
        identity is app id, machine id and actor id, never an app property key.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/app.ts.
    O2  Setup and readiness
        runtimeSetup({ app, services?, persistence? }).construct() is synchronous and inert.
        services is an Effect Layer that must provide every service the app's operation adapters
        need, lazy entries included; a missing service is a type error. Each service is acquired
        once per Runtime.

        ready() is the one readiness Effect. The first call starts the bootstrap; concurrent and
        later callers share its result, including a failure (no implicit retry). Interrupting one
        waiter interrupts only that wait. Bootstrap order:

        1. With persistence in a browser that has Web Locks, try the (app, scope) lock without
        waiting. If another tab holds it, report SecondTabNoPersistence and run without restore
        or capture. Without Web Locks (server, insecure origin) skip this.
        2. Acquire storage, then read and validate persisted data before any app service starts.
        Invalid data is discarded with a diagnostic (@plan.FlowPersistence.O5).
        3. Acquire the remaining services, install restored data and actors (providers before
        consumers), establish silent context baselines, then open the Runtime to callers.

        A service acquisition failure fails ready() with FlowError and its Cause, releases what
        was acquired in reverse order, and leaves no partial actor or evidence. Before ready
        succeeds, ensureActor and createActor reject with NotReady; React's pre-attach handles
        (@O4) are the only exception. runtime.runPromise runs an Effect with the installed
        services; there is no runPromiseExit.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/setup.ts, app/host.ts, app/persistence.ts.
    O3  Actor identity
        actorRef(machine, id, { persist?, retain?, context? }) is inert. id is a string or a
        readonly array of strings; arrays encode unambiguously, so ["a/b", "c"] and ["a", "b/c"]
        are different actors. Two refs with the same machine and id but different persist, retain
        or context reject with FlowError and change nothing.

        runtime.ensureActor(ref, { input }) returns the one shared actor for that identity,
        creating or restoring it. The first accepted input initializes it; later ensures never
        reset memory, and a different later input reports a diagnostic. A failed admission leaves
        nothing behind, so a later ensure may retry with new input. An input-free machine is
        ensured with no options. runtime.createActor(machine, { input }) always creates a new
        local actor that cannot persist or be a send target. Handles expose send, getSnapshot and
        snapshots.

        Disposing and then ensuring the same ref creates a new incarnation. Ensure while disposing
        waits for cleanup, then creates. No outcome, schedule, issue or send from an old
        incarnation ever reaches a new one.

        A use is an attached React consumer, a live snapshots subscription or a bound context
        consumer. With retain, a shared actor is disposed retain after its last use ends;
        a new use cancels that. A provider with bound consumers never expires. Without retain a
        shared actor lives until explicit or Runtime disposal.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.ref.ts, app/host.ts.
    O4  Mailboxes and send
        Each actor processes events one at a time in FIFO order; a send during a turn is queued,
        never run inside the current turn. Each actor queues at most 1,024 pending events: an
        external send beyond that throws AdmissionOverloaded and changes nothing. send validates
        the event's tag and payload; an internal, unknown or invalid event throws InvalidEvent and
        starts no turn. Sends to a disposing or disposed actor are dropped with SendTargetAbsent.
        Internal sources (outcomes, schedules, streams, context) obey the same bound and never
        silently drop a fact.

        Before attach, a React ensuring hook returns a handle reading a pure snapshot (default
        states, initial(input), Pending lanes, current provider context). Render registers and
        starts nothing. Up to 64 sends are buffered and delivered once, in order, on attach; the
        65th throws AdmissionOverloaded. An abandoned handle stays inert.

        An action send(ref, event) delivers a public event to a shared actor after the sender's
        turn commits, as a later turn of the target. If the target is absent or disposing the
        event is dropped with SendTargetAbsent; if it is full, AdmissionOverloaded. Both are
        reported on the sender and never roll back its turn. Several sends keep their order.

        Every publication is one complete cut: a reader never sees an actor's new snapshot with
        old store data or the reverse. A new snapshots subscriber gets current truth first; slow
        subscribers may skip intermediate snapshots. Snapshots carry no revisions or raw Cause.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts.
    O5  Context propagation
        Provider.select(selector) reads one complete provider snapshot. A context slot binds to
        one provider for the consumer's lifetime. Context reads form an acyclic upward graph;
        a cycle, missing or incompatible provider rejects at admission. The first context
        baseline is silent: onContext does not fire for it.

        When a provider changes, each affected consumer receives at most one context update per
        wave, computed from the latest complete provider snapshots, and never a mix of new and
        stale values from the same cause (diamonds included). Slots compare with Equal.equals; an
        equal value causes no update. A context update keeps its place in the FIFO queue.

        A consumer selector that throws keeps that consumer's last valid context, publishes one
        issue, and retries only on a newer provider snapshot; the provider and other consumers are
        unaffected. Persistence capture never observes a half-propagated wave
        (ConcurrentCapture, retried by the Runtime).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/queue/queue.machine.ts.
    O6  Lifecycle and disposal
        Public lifecycle is active, disposing or disposed. runtime.disposeActor(actor) disposes
        the actor for every user. Disposing a provider while consumers are bound rejects with
        FlowError naming them. Actor and Runtime disposal are idempotent: repeated calls share one
        result.

        On disposal, queued events that have not started are dropped without running. Schedules
        cancel, activities stop, and invoked commits are allowed to settle. Runtime disposal is
        bounded: after 5 seconds a commit still in flight becomes unknown with its Attempt, and
        its late result is ignored. A cleanup failure rejects dispose, quarantines the ref
        (later ensures report the same failure) and keeps the full Cause; it is never reported as
        success. Runtime disposal finishes only after the final persistence write is acknowledged.
        The terminal snapshot keeps the last committed state and memory; disposal runs no machine
        event.

        runtime.dispose({ forget: true }) also deletes the scope's stored records
        (@plan.FlowPersistence.O5); plain dispose keeps them.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/host.ts.
    O7  Isolation
        Runtimes share no mutable state: actors, store, services, storage, clock or evidence.
        Handles from one Runtime are rejected by another; nested React providers use the nearest
        Runtime. A different user is a new Runtime and persistence scope (a keyed FlowProvider).
        Every server request and Story run constructs its own Runtime; a module-level server
        Runtime is never used. Story storage is always fresh memory storage (@plan.Testing.O7).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/next/providers.tsx.
decisions
    D1  The app is a closed list of machines
        Lazy entries name machines the app already declares; nothing registers at runtime. This
        keeps the structural representation complete for tools.
    D2  Wiring lives on refs and app defaults, never at call sites
        One place declares where an actor's context comes from, so tools can draw the graph.
    D3  Actor sends are visible, asynchronous and committed first
        A send is a declared edge and a later turn of the target; a target failure never undoes
        the sender's committed turn.
    D4  Three public lifecycle states
        active, disposing, disposed. No suspension; React keeps actors alive with retain and
        grace, not a suspended state.
constraints
    C1  One execution path
        Hosts, requests, React, Stories and the CLI use the same Runtime; fixtures replace
        services, never the kernel.
verification
    V1  Construction: closed-list errors, unambiguous id parts and inert constructors.
    V2  Services: a missing adapter service is a type error; each service is acquired once.
    V3  Readiness: storage before validation, shared failure, interrupted waiter, second-tab
        refusal, no partial handles after failure.
    V4  Identity: first input wins with a diagnostic, conflicting refs reject, re-ensure after
        dispose ignores every late result of the old incarnation, retain expiry on the TestClock.
    V5  Mailboxes: 1,024 and 64 bounds, invalid events, FIFO, send failures owned by the sender.
    V6  Context: diamond and chain waves give one update from latest values; a throwing selector
        is isolated.
    V7  Disposal and isolation: provider-with-consumers rejection, idempotent dispose, 5-second
        bound with unknown commits, quarantine on cleanup failure, no sharing between Runtimes.
questions
    Q1  How does a synchronous ensure wait for a disposing incarnation?
        Recommended: return a pending handle that reads the pre-attach snapshot and attaches
        after cleanup; settle it in the S1 type spike.
    Q2  Can one services Layer be acquired storage-first?
        Recommended: prove it against Effect 4.0.5 in S1; if not, ask the owner whether to
        acquire the whole Layer before restore validation.
    Q3  Deferred to build time
        Exact UTF-8 name limits and id byte vectors; counter headroom rules; internal producer
        back-pressure policy; evidence ordering details; lifecycle evidence causes; the private
        shutdown budget split. See references/full/Runtime.full.plan.txt.
